.policy "python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async" revision 1
.description "Don't call a sync helper that sleeps, does network I/O, runs a subprocess or starts an event loop from an async def; run it in a thread or make it async."
.language python
.mode log
.scope include "**/*.py"
// Rerail's own rule, not a port. A coroutine runs on the event loop's thread until its next await: a blocking call
// anywhere below it, also in a plain helper in another module, stalls every other task on the loop until it returns.
// It reports, in an async def's own frame (not a nested def, lambda or class body) and outside test code:
//   - a call (BlockingCallReached) whose target, by the call graph (python/calls, across files), is a sync def of the
//     run that blocks its thread, itself or through a chain of calls through other sync defs: time.sleep, the
//     sync requests/httpx module functions, urlopen, subprocess.run/call/check_call/check_output/getoutput/
//     getstatusoutput, os.system, os.popen, os.wait*, socket.create_connection/getaddrinfo/gethostbyname,
//     smtplib.SMTP (lib: pp_blocking, a name list: core misc2-async has none to reuse);
//   - a call (LoopStartReached) whose target reaches, the same way, a call that starts an event loop and waits for it:
//     asyncio.run(), <loop>.run_until_complete(), async_to_sync(f)() (lib: pp_loop_entry). With a loop already running
//     in the thread these raise RuntimeError: a crash, not a stall;
//   - such a loop start written in the async def itself (LoopStartInAsync).
// A step of the chain (lib: pp_step_at) is a sure call target; a def under decorators that keep calling it in the
// caller's thread, imported from outside the run (functools.lru_cache/cache, cachetools, tenacity.retry, backoff,
// retrying, stamina: a name list, since their code is not in the run), which the call program leaves open; or the def
// a decorator of the run wraps, when its wrapper calls the wrapped function in its own frame (`@wraps(f) def w(*a):
// return f(*a)`, also from a decorator factory `@retrying(3)`). One call gives one finding, naming the first blocking
// site of the reached def with the least symbol.
//   services/http.py: def fetch(url): return requests.get(url, timeout=5)
//   routes.py:        async def page(url): r = fetch(url)                          reported at fetch(url)
//   routes.py:        async def page(url): r = await asyncio.to_thread(fetch, url)  not reported: fetch runs in a thread
//   services/http.py: @tenacity.retry(stop=stop_after_attempt(3)) def get(url): return requests.get(url, timeout=5)
//   routes.py:        async def page(url): r = get(url)                            reported: the retry runs it here
//   services/loops.py: def refresh_now(): asyncio.run(refresh())
//   routes.py:        async def reload(): refresh_now()                            reported (LoopStartReached)
// Not reported: a blocking call written in the async def itself (the python pack's async rules report it); a helper
// passed to a thread (asyncio.to_thread, run_in_executor, anyio.to_thread.run_sync, sync_to_async(f)()) or decorated
// with sync_to_async/database_sync_to_async, or by a decorator of the run that hands it to a thread or executor; a
// chain through an async def (calling one only makes a coroutine), through a call whose target the call program cannot
// settle (call_open), or through a decorator imported from outside the run that is not in the list above; a call in a
// nested def, lambda or class body; names that only look alike (`from svc import requests` with svc.requests a
// module of the run, trio.sleep, asyncio.sleep, httpx.AsyncClient); test files.
// Loop starts that cannot raise (lib: pp_loop_guarded) are not reported: one in the `except RuntimeError` of a `try`
// that calls asyncio.get_running_loop(), in the branch `if loop is None:` or `if not loop.is_running():` leads to,
// or after nest_asyncio.apply(). A dual-mode helper starts its loop only when no loop runs:
//   def run_sync(coro):
//       try: asyncio.get_running_loop()
//       except RuntimeError: return asyncio.run(coro)                 not reported, called from a coroutine or not
//       return asyncio.ensure_future(coro)
// Startup code is not reported for a blocking call (a loop start raises all the same, and is): it runs before the
// server takes requests or after it stops, so no request waits on it. Startup code is a lifespan (an
// asynccontextmanager passed as `lifespan=`), an on_event("startup"/"shutdown") or add_event_handler("startup", f)
// handler, an entry of `on_startup=[...]`/`on_shutdown=[...]`, and a coroutine only startup code awaits, at any depth:
//   app.py:    async def lifespan(app): await server.init_async(); yield
//   server.py: async def init_async(self): await self._prepare()      _prepare: prepare_sandbox()  not reported
// A coroutine is startup code only when every live call that may run it (its call targets, and for an open call each
// async def of the callee's name) is awaited directly in startup code, no other live code names it (passed as a
// callback, to functools.partial), and it has no decorator but the known ones above. One spawned as a task from the
// lifespan (`asyncio.create_task(poll())`) runs while serving, and is reported.
// `self.m()` in a method of class K follows K's own m (or the one K inherits), not a subclass's override: a base's
// `async def` that calls `self.m()` is judged with the base's m. A blocking call that runs once per process is not
// reported: under `if self._x is None:` (or a module global) whose body sets that same name (`git --version` probed
// once and cached), or in a def memoized by lru_cache/cache/cachetools that takes no argument but self or cls, and
// whatever it calls. A memoized def with arguments blocks for each new key, and is reported.
// Gaps: file I/O (open(), Path.read_text()) is left out on purpose: short local reads are common in async code and
// rarely what stalls a loop. Sync clients reached through an object (requests.Session().get, smtplib.SMTP().sendmail,
// a sync DB driver) are not followed: only the module functions and constructors above count. A class body nested in
// the async def does run at once, but is skipped with defs and lambdas. A subclass that inherits a base's async def
// and overrides only the sync m it calls is not reported, nor is `self.m()` where m is under a decorator of the run.
// A loop start guarded by a test this rule does not read (a flag, a helper returning whether a loop runs) is reported.
// Startup code the rule cannot tie to a lifespan is reported: a main coroutine run by asyncio.run(main()) or a
// tornado IOLoop.run_sync(partial(app.launch, argv)), whose first awaits run before the server listens; telling which
// awaits precede the first spawned task needs the order of statements across coroutines. The message says when to
// leave it.

#include "lib/python_practice.rerail"

// The walk is over symbols (decision 0677: no node crosses files): a def by its symbol, a call by its term (lib:
// pp_async_start, pp_sync_reach). Each def's first blocking call and first loop start are stated in its own file
// (pbr_ran, pbr_loop_ran); a finding reads its call's node in its own file and names the site by its path and line.
// Of the reached defs that block, the finding names the one with the least symbol.
.violation BlockingCallReached(at: node, g: symbol, what: symbol, p: symbol, l: number)
    "`{g}()` blocks the event loop (`{what}` at {p}:{l}), stalling every task. Await `asyncio.to_thread({g}, ...)` or make it async; leave it if it runs only before serving."
BlockingCallReached(c, x, what, p, l) :- pbr_site(c, ct, fq), !pbr_startup_def(fq), pbr_first(ct, hq),
    pbr_ran(hq, what, p, l), field(c, "function", fn), text(fn, x).
.violation LoopStartReached(at: node, g: symbol, what: symbol, p: symbol, l: number)
    "`{g}()` starts an event loop (`{what}` at {p}:{l}) while one runs in this thread: it raises RuntimeError. Await the coroutine instead, or give the helper an async twin."
LoopStartReached(c, x, what, p, l) :- pbr_site(c, ct, _), pbr_loop_first(ct, hq), pbr_loop_ran(hq, what, p, l),
    field(c, "function", fn), text(fn, x).
.violation LoopStartInAsync(at: node, what: symbol, fx: symbol)
    "`{what}` in `async def {fx}` starts an event loop while one already runs in this thread: it raises RuntimeError. Await the coroutine directly."
LoopStartInAsync(c, what, fx) :- pp_loop_entry(c, f, what), pp_async_def(f), pp_live(c), !pp_loop_guarded(c),
    def_name(f, fx).

// pbr_site(c, ct, f): call c, whose term is ct, is in async def f's own frame (f its symbol), outside test code.
.decl pbr_site(c: node, ct: symbol, f: symbol)
pbr_site(c, ct, fq) :- kind(c, "call"), pp_frame(c, f), pp_async_def(f), pp_live(c), pt_def(f, fq), pt_tm(c, ct).
// pbr_start(ct, g): pp_async_start, with `self.m()` in a method of class K going only to what K resolves m to. The
// call program also answers a subclass's override (a late-bound self), which runs only for that subclass; when the
// subclass overrides the async def too, the base's async def never runs the override.
.decl pbr_start(ct: symbol, g: symbol)
pbr_start(ct, g) :- pp_async_start(ct, _, g), !pbr_self_call_at(ct, _, _).
pbr_start(ct, g) :- pp_async_start(ct, _, g), pbr_self_call_at(ct, kq, m), mro_method_sym(kq, m, g).
// pbr_self_call_at(ct, kq, m): the call ct is `self.m(...)` in a method of class kq. Stated in its file.
.decl pbr_self_call_at(ct: symbol, kq: symbol, m: symbol)
pbr_self_call_at(ct, kq, m) :- kind(c, "call"), field(c, "function", fn), attr_of(fn, r, m), kind(r, "identifier"),
    text(r, "self"), pp_frame(c, f), nearest(f, k, scope), class_def(k, kq), pt_tm(c, ct).
// pbr_each(g, h): pp_sync_reach, but not into a def that runs once per process (pbr_once_def): what blocks each time
// g is called.
.decl pbr_each(g: symbol, h: symbol)
pbr_each(g, g) :- pbr_start(_, g), !pbr_once_def(g).
pbr_each(g, h) :- pbr_each(g, x), pp_sync_step(x, h), !pbr_once_def(h).
// pbr_once_def(g): g is memoized (lru_cache, cache, cachetools) and takes no argument but self or cls. Stated in g's
// file.
.decl pbr_once_def(g: symbol)
pbr_once_def(gq) :- decorator(g, e), pp_decorator_name(e, q), pp_memoizer(q), !pbr_keyed(g), pt_def(g, gq).
.decl pbr_keyed(g: node)
pbr_keyed(g) :- param(g, x, _), x != "self", x != "cls".
// pbr_first(c, h): of the defs that block each time the start c reaches them, the one with the least symbol (a `min`
// over symbols would order their ids). pbr_loop_first(c, h): the same for the defs that start a loop.
.decl pbr_hit(ct: symbol, h: symbol)
pbr_hit(ct, hq) :- pbr_start(ct, g), pbr_each(g, hq), pbr_ran(hq, _, _, _).
.decl pbr_first(ct: symbol, h: symbol)
pbr_first(ct, hq) :- pbr_hit(ct, hq), !pbr_later(ct, hq).
.decl pbr_later(ct: symbol, h: symbol)
pbr_later(ct, hq) :- pbr_hit(ct, hq), pbr_hit(ct, iq), iq < hq.
.decl pbr_loop_hit(ct: symbol, h: symbol)
pbr_loop_hit(ct, hq) :- pbr_start(ct, g), pp_sync_reach(g, hq), pbr_loop_ran(hq, _, _, _).
.decl pbr_loop_first(ct: symbol, h: symbol)
pbr_loop_first(ct, hq) :- pbr_loop_hit(ct, hq), !pbr_loop_later(ct, hq).
.decl pbr_loop_later(ct: symbol, h: symbol)
pbr_loop_later(ct, hq) :- pbr_loop_hit(ct, hq), pbr_loop_hit(ct, iq), iq < hq.
// pbr_ran(h, what, p, l): def h's first blocking call that can run more than once (pbr_blocking), `what`, at line l
// of path p. pbr_loop_ran(h, what, p, l): its first loop start that can raise. Stated in h's file.
.decl pbr_ran(h: symbol, what: symbol, p: symbol, l: number)
pbr_ran(hq, what, p, l) :- pbr_blocking(_, h), o = min y : { pbr_blocking(y, h) }, pp_blocking(o, _, what),
    pt_def(h, hq), line(o, l), docof(o, d), doc(d, p, _).
.decl pbr_loop_ran(h: symbol, what: symbol, p: symbol, l: number)
pbr_loop_ran(hq, what, p, l) :- pbr_loop_start(_, h), o = min y : { pbr_loop_start(y, h) }, pp_loop_entry(o, _, what),
    pt_def(h, hq), line(o, l), docof(o, d), doc(d, p, _).
.decl pbr_loop_start(y: node, h: node)
pbr_loop_start(y, h) :- pp_loop_entry(y, h, _), !pp_loop_guarded(y).

// ---- startup code -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The walk is over symbols too: each file states its roots, its calls of async defs and the names it reads.
// pbr_lifespan(f): the roots: a lifespan, an on_event/add_event_handler startup or shutdown handler, an entry of
// `on_startup=[...]` or `on_shutdown=[...]` (Starlette, FastAPI). A root named in one file (`lifespan=lifespan`,
// `add_event_handler("startup", init)`) is a def of its qualified name, in any file.
.decl pbr_lifespan(f: symbol)
pbr_lifespan(fq) :- pbr_named_root(q, "cm"), pp_def_q(q, fq), pbr_cm_sym(fq).
pbr_lifespan(fq) :- pbr_named_root(q, "async"), pp_def_q(q, fq), pp_async_sym(fq).
pbr_lifespan(fq) :- pp_async_def(f), decorator(f, e), kind(e, "call"), field(e, "function", fn),
    attr_of(fn, _, "on_event"), posarg(e, 0, s), text(s, t), (contains("startup", t) ; contains("shutdown", t)),
    pt_def(f, fq).
// pbr_named_root(q, k): q is passed as a root: as `lifespan=` (k "cm", a contextmanager), or to add_event_handler or
// in an `on_startup=`/`on_shutdown=` list (k "async", an async def). Stated in the file that passes it.
.decl pbr_named_root(q: symbol, k: symbol)
pbr_named_root(q, "cm") :- kind(a, "keyword_argument"), field(a, "name", n), text(n, "lifespan"), field(a, "value", v),
    sym_of(v, q).
pbr_named_root(q, "async") :- kind(e, "call"), field(e, "function", fn), attr_of(fn, _, "add_event_handler"),
    posarg(e, 0, s), text(s, t), (contains("startup", t) ; contains("shutdown", t)), posarg(e, 1, r), sym_of(r, q).
pbr_named_root(q, "async") :- pp_kwarg(e, k, v), (k = "on_startup" ; k = "on_shutdown"), kind(v, "list"),
    child(v, r, _), sym_of(r, q).
.decl pbr_cm_sym(f: symbol)
pbr_cm_sym(fq) :- pp_cm_def(f), pt_def(f, fq).
// pbr_startup_def(f): a root, or an async def awaited from one, any depth, that nothing else may run (!pbr_runtime).
.decl pbr_startup_def(f: symbol)
pbr_startup_def(f) :- pbr_lifespan(f).
pbr_startup_def(g) :- pbr_caller(ct, g), pbr_call_in(ct, f), pbr_startup_def(f), !pbr_runtime(g).
// pbr_call_in(ct, f): call ct is in def f's own frame. Stated in its file.
.decl pbr_call_in(ct: symbol, f: symbol)
pbr_call_in(ct, fq) :- kind(c, "call"), pp_frame(c, f), pt_def(f, fq), pt_tm(c, ct).
// pbr_caller(ct, g): live call ct may run async def g: ct's call target, or, when ct is open, any async def of its
// callee's name (`server.init_async()` on a global the program cannot type).
.decl pbr_caller(ct: symbol, g: symbol)
pbr_caller(ct, g) :- pbr_live_call(ct), pp_target_at(ct, g), pp_async_sym(g).
pbr_caller(ct, g) :- pbr_open_leaf(ct, x), pbr_async_named(x, g).
.decl pbr_live_call(ct: symbol)
pbr_live_call(ct) :- kind(c, "call"), pp_live(c), pt_tm(c, ct).
// pbr_open_leaf(ct, x): live open call ct calls a callee whose last name is x. Stated in its file.
.decl pbr_open_leaf(ct: symbol, x: symbol)
pbr_open_leaf(ct, x) :- call_open(c), pp_live(c), field(c, "function", fn), pbr_leaf(fn, x), pt_tm(c, ct).
.decl pbr_leaf(e: node, x: symbol)
pbr_leaf(e, x) :- kind(e, "identifier"), text(e, x).
pbr_leaf(e, x) :- attr_of(e, _, x).
// pbr_async_named(x, g): async def g is named x.
.decl pbr_async_named(x: symbol, g: symbol)
pbr_async_named(x, gq) :- pp_async_def(g), def_name(g, x), pt_def(g, gq).
// pbr_runtime(g): async def g, not a root, may run while requests are served: nothing in the run calls it (the
// framework does), it has a decorator not known to keep it as it is, live code names it other than as a call's
// callee, or a live call that may run it is not awaited in a coroutine or sits in a coroutine that may so run.
.decl pbr_runtime(g: symbol)
pbr_runtime(g) :- pp_async_sym(g), !pbr_lifespan(g), !pbr_called(g).
pbr_runtime(g) :- pbr_odd_async(g), !pbr_lifespan(g).
pbr_runtime(g) :- pbr_async_named(x, g), !pbr_lifespan(g), pbr_named(x).
pbr_runtime(g) :- pbr_caller(ct, g), !pbr_lifespan(g), !pbr_awaited_here(ct).
pbr_runtime(g) :- pbr_caller(ct, g), !pbr_lifespan(g), pbr_call_in(ct, f), pbr_runtime(f).
.decl pbr_called(g: symbol)
pbr_called(g) :- pbr_caller(_, g).
// pbr_odd_async(g): async def g has a decorator not known to call it in the caller's thread. Stated in g's file.
.decl pbr_odd_async(g: symbol)
pbr_odd_async(gq) :- pp_async_def(g), pp_odd_decorator(g), pt_def(g, gq).
// pbr_awaited_here(ct): the call ct is the operand of `await` in an async def's own frame. Stated in its file.
.decl pbr_awaited_here(ct: symbol)
pbr_awaited_here(ct) :- awaited(c), pp_frame(c, f), pp_async_def(f), pt_tm(c, ct).
// pbr_named(x): live code reads the name x other than as a call's callee, a def's name or an import: a reference
// that may hand the coroutine to code the rule does not see (`partial(app.launch, argv)`, `create_task(x)`). Each
// file states the names it reads so.
.decl pbr_named(x: symbol)
pbr_named(x) :- kind(n, "identifier"), text(n, x), pp_live(n), !pbr_callee_name(n), !pbr_def_name_node(n),
    !inside(n, _, pbr_import_kind).
.decl pbr_callee_name(n: node)
pbr_callee_name(n) :- kind(c, "call"), field(c, "function", n).
pbr_callee_name(n) :- kind(c, "call"), field(c, "function", fn), kind(fn, "attribute"), field(fn, "attribute", n).
.decl pbr_def_name_node(n: node)
pbr_def_name_node(n) :- kind(d, k), (k = "function_definition" ; k = "class_definition"), field(d, "name", n).
.decl pbr_import_kind(k: symbol)
pbr_import_kind("import_statement"). pbr_import_kind("import_from_statement").

// ---- once per process ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// pbr_blocking(y, h): y is a blocking call in h's own frame that can run more than once: not under a once-guard.
.decl pbr_blocking(y: node, h: node)
pbr_blocking(y, h) :- pp_blocking(y, h, _), !pbr_once(y).
// pbr_once(y): y sits in the body of `if <name> is None:` that assigns <name> (`self._git_available`, a module
// global): a lazy one-time probe whose result is cached, so it blocks once per process, not per request.
.decl pbr_once(y: node)
pbr_once(y) :- pp_blocking(y, _, _), kind(i, "if_statement"), field(i, "consequence", b), within(y, b),
    field(i, "condition", cnd), kind(cnd, "comparison_operator"), text(cnd, ct), contains(" is None", ct),
    child(cnd, l, 0), (kind(l, "attribute") ; kind(l, "identifier")), text(l, lt), kind(s, "assignment"),
    within(s, b), field(s, "left", t), text(t, lt).

// The cases, `cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/`, read as one repository: each line ending in
// `# caught` is reported, and no other line.
.test cases exactly
    .fixture "svc/__init__.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/__init__.py"
    .fixture "svc/decorated.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/decorated.py"
    .fixture "svc/decorators.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/decorators.py"
    .fixture "svc/dual.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/dual.py"
    .fixture "svc/lookalikes.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/lookalikes.py"
    .fixture "svc/main.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/main.py"
    .fixture "svc/providers.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/providers.py"
    .fixture "svc/requests.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/requests.py"
    .fixture "svc/routes.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/routes.py"
    .fixture "svc/safe.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/safe.py"
    .fixture "svc/server.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/server.py"
    .fixture "svc/services/__init__.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/services/__init__.py"
    .fixture "svc/services/guards.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/services/guards.py"
    .fixture "svc/services/http.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/services/http.py"
    .fixture "svc/services/loops.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/services/loops.py"
    .fixture "svc/services/shell.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/services/shell.py"
    .fixture "svc/services/wrapped.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/services/wrapped.py"
    .fixture "svc/startup.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/startup.py"
    .fixture "svc/tests/__init__.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/tests/__init__.py"
    .fixture "svc/tests/test_routes.py" from "../cases/python-practice-blocking-call-reached-from-async/svc/tests/test_routes.py"
